WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.547
In this example, we import the statistics module using the alias stats.

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:07.902
We start with a list of scores.

00:00:07.902 --> 00:00:14.547
Then we calculate the average using stats.mean and the median using stats.median.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:18.872
Notice how the alias still tells us where the function comes from.

00:00:18.872 --> 00:00:24.642
When we see stats.mean, we know that mean is coming from the statistics module.

00:00:24.642 --> 00:00:28.222
The name is shorter, but it is still meaningful.

00:00:28.222 --> 00:00:30.942
We also use min and max, which are built-in Python functions.

00:00:30.942 --> 00:00:37.662
These do not need an import because Python already knows them.

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:43.522
Before running the code, look at the list of scores and think about what the output should include.

00:00:43.522 --> 00:00:48.117
You should see the original scores, the average, the median, and the range.

00:00:48.117 --> 00:00:54.577
The range is calculated by subtracting the lowest score from the highest score.

00:00:54.577 --> 00:00:59.517
After running the cell, add one more score to the list and run it again.

00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:03.392
Watch how the average, median, and range change.

00:01:03.392 --> 00:01:07.057
This example connects imports to analytics.

00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:12.872
Many analytical tasks rely on reusable functions that summarize or transform data.

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:16.627
Importing the right module keeps the code short and readable.
